      "type": "function",
      "function": {
        "name": "get_potential_metadata",
        "description": "Structured metadata string describing the potential file.\n\nThe returned string includes the type of interatomic potential and a list of chemical elements that it supports. This helps in choosing suitable potentials for simulations involving specific atoms.\n\nWhen to use: When to use this tool:\n    - Use when you need to quickly determine the type, supported elements or the LAMMPS pair style of a LAMMPS potential file based on its filename.\n    - Best suited for selecting an appropriate potential file for a specific molecular dynamics (MD) simulation without reading or parsing the full file contents.\n    - Recommended for gaining a fast, structured overview of a potential file's applicability to specific element combinations or simulation scenarios.\n\nHow this tool works:\n    - Extracts the file name from the provided file path\n    - Matches it against a set of known file names\n    - Returns a structured metadata string for recognized files\n    - Raises a ValueError if the file name is unrecognized\n\nWorkflow: Typical workflow integration:\n    1.  Select the potential files that might be relevant to your simulation task. \n    2.  Use this tool to retrieve metadata for each potential file based on its filename. This will help you quickly identify which potentials are suitable for your simulation needs. \n    3.  Based on the metadata returned, choose the appropriate potential file for your simulation setup, and run the simulation using the potential file and the tool `run_lammps`.",
        "parameters": {
          "type": "object",
          "properties": {
            "file_path": {
              "description": "Absolute path to the potential file. This is the absolute path to a LAMMPS-compatible potential file (e.g., ReaxFF or EAM formats). The file name is used to determine metadata, so it must match one of the known patterns.",
              "type": "string"
            }
          },
          "required": [
            "file_path"
          ]
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "function",
      "function": {
        "name": "run_lammps",
        "description": "Message indicating simulation completion with log file location.\n\nOn success, returns a message confirming the simulation run, the path to the latest input script used, and the corresponding log file. The log file contains detailed runtime diagnostics and output for verification.\n\nWhen to use: When to use this tool:\n    - Use when you need to execute a LAMMPS molecular dynamics simulation using a predefined input script.\n    - Use to run a molecular dynamics simulation without needing to manually start LAMMPS or handle the command line interface.\n    - Recommended for production simulations, high-throughput screening, and automated pipelines where simulation setup is complete and ready to run.\n\nHow this tool works:\n    - The input LAMMPS script (typically ending in `.in`) is passed to the remote backend.\n    - The tool constructs a log file name by replacing the script's extension with `.log`.\n    - A remote function is invoked with the input file, the log file path.\n\nWorkflow: Typical workflow integration:\n    1.  Ensure you have a valid LAMMPS input script ready for execution.\n    This script should contain all necessary simulation parameters, atom definitions, force fields, and commands. Additionally, ensure that the .data file is in place. You can create one using the function `convert_structure_to_lammps_data`.  \n    2.  Use this tool to run the LAMMPS simulation by providing the path to the input script. The tool will handle the remote execution and log file generation. \n    3.  After the simulation completes, check the generated log file for results, diagnostics, and any errors. The log file will be named based on the input script, with a `.log` extension. You can then proceed to analyze the results or use the output data in subsequent steps of your workflow.",
        "parameters": {
          "type": "object",
          "properties": {
            "input_file": {
              "description": "Path to the LAMMPS input script file. This parameter specifies the absolute or relative path to the input script used by LAMMPS. The script typically contains simulation settings such as atom style, force field parameters, boundary conditions, and compute directives. The file must be in LAMMPS-compatible format (`.in` extension is conventional but not required) and should not require interactive input during execution.",
              "type": "string"
            }
          },
          "required": [
            "input_file"
          ]
        }
      }
    }
  ],
